WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.877
Errors: who should see the failure?

2
00:00:02.157 --> 00:00:06.381
A tool can fail in three ways, and the SDK treats each differently.

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:12.336
The SDK docs offer one deciding question: could a smarter model have avoided this?

4
00:00:12.616 --> 00:00:14.259
If yes, raise ToolError.

5
00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:20.661
The call returns a normal result with is_error true and your message in the content, where the model reads it.

6
00:00:20.941 --> 00:00:31.864
A wrong issue id, a status change the workflow forbids, an upstream API that timed out: the model chose the call, so it should see the consequence and get the chance to fix it.

7
00:00:32.144 --> 00:00:38.843
Write the message as an instruction, as you did in Studio 1: “No issue HAR-999.

8
00:00:39.123 --> 00:00:44.093
Known ids include HAR-101 through HAR-107.

9
00:00:44.373 --> 00:00:46.165
Use search_issues to find one.

10
00:00:46.445 --> 00:00:46.743
”.

11
00:00:47.023 --> 00:00:49.839
If no, raise MCPError (from mcp ).

12
00:00:50.119 --> 00:00:55.410
The whole request fails with a JSON-RPC error that goes to the host, not the model.

13
00:00:55.690 --> 00:01:02.346
Use it when the request itself should be rejected, for instance when the client lacks a capability your tool depends on.

14
00:01:02.626 --> 00:01:04.866
Any other exception is treated as a crash.

15
00:01:05.146 --> 00:01:09.839
The model learns only that the call failed; the traceback goes to your server’s log.

16
00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:18.012
That is deliberate: an exception message can leak internals, such as a SQL fragment or a file path, to a remote caller.

17
00:01:18.292 --> 00:01:34.058
Resources draw the same line with their own exceptions: raise ResourceNotFoundError when a templated URI is well formed but names something that does not exist, and the client receives the protocol’s not-found error with the URI attached.

18
00:01:34.338 --> 00:01:40.524
And one rule from the SDK docs that is easy to break: never return an error message from a tool.

19
00:01:40.804 --> 00:01:43.514
A returned string is a success, whatever it says.
